  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 146 AM EDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis and Forecast:\n\nFun little setup potentially for today, definitely has that cool \nseason smell to it. Stout upper low across Ontario to our north (and \nlow pressure system), with impressive short wave diving across the \nsouthern periphery of the broader trough. Consequently, heights \nlower, temperatures aloft decrease, and lapse rates steepen across \nnorthern MI. Additionally, upper level jet stream dives across the \nUpper Midwest, putting the best juxtaposition of upper support and \ninstability (couple hundred joules possible) roughly across the\nTip of the Mitt vicinity and northward. Thus, scattered showers\nand storms will be possible this afternoon across this region, \nspreading southward with time this evening and early overnight. \nThe steep low to mid level lapse rates and ~30+ KTs aloft \nsuggest there will actually be the potential for strong winds \nassuming these scattered showers and thunderstorms are able to \nremain robust as they move into the NWS Gaylord county warning \narea. Small hail is not completely out of the question but the \nwind potential is something that should be focused on. \nAdditionally, looks like some of our neighbors threw in \nwaterspout potential, will try and follow suit. Showers and a \nfew storms remain possible into at least the first half of the \novernight hours as heights aloft remain depressed and any low \nlevel convergence associated with the low pressure well to our \nnorth push into the region. \n\nContinued depressed heights and cooler air aloft over the region on \nThursday will result in a few additional showers, albeit the main \nshort wave will be ejecting east and the cold pool aloft will be \nretreating to the north of N MI. Still, lapse rates may be steep \nenough for a few additional showers/low topped convection through \nthe day on Thursday, again focusing across the E UP and adjacent \nlocations of northern lower. Not as thermodynamically inclined as \nthe Wednesday though. Temperatures will also be a little cooler, \nwith highs in the upper 50s to low 60s most locations.\n\nThis weekend will feature rising heights aloft and increasing \nsouthwesterly winds. Thus, despite any brief low confidence showery \nactivity Friday night, temperatures will rise into the 70s this \nweekend. Current forecast suggests a very low chance for \nprecipitation, but might be worth keeping an eye on a low pressure \nsystem ejects out of the Deep South and moves northeast later on \nSunday (most guidance and ENS keep this feature to the east of N \nMI). Early next week will feature a cool down as a frontal boundary \nand active weather works into the Great Lakes region.",
